    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class (the other files are a base and a helper). The full suite is green \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS, Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI redesigned the owner identity key and duplicate detection to satisfy the identity-key-v2 spec.\n\n**`OwnerIdentities.java`** \u2014 `identityKey(Owner)` now returns the lower-case hex **SHA-256** of `normalizedTelephone + \"|\" + lowerEmail + \"|\" + soundex(lastName)` (previously a plaintext `telephone|email|householdId` string). Added a public American **`soundex(String)`** implementation (first letter + 3 digits, with the standard vowel-reset and transparent-h/w rules) used by both the key and the soft-match. Removed the now-unused `normalizeHouseholdId` helper.\n\n**`CheckUniqueIdentity.java`** \u2014 no logic change needed (it delegates to `identityKey`), so the 409 hard-duplicate check now keys off the new hash, still ignoring soft-deleted owners and still bypassed by `sharesHousehold=true`. Because telephone is in the key, two owners with the same last name + postcode but different telephones no longer collide, and the postcode-based household-duplicate 409 (formerly via `householdId` in the key) is subsumed. The email-domain blocklist still runs first in `ValidateOwnerFields`. Updated the javadoc.\n\n**`CheckPossibleDuplicate.java`** \u2014 the soft match now triggers when the **identityKey differs** but **soundex(lastName) and postcode match** (previously exact lastName + postcode + different telephone). So same-household/different-telephone registrations are created and flagged `possibleDuplicate` rather than rejected.\n\n**`openapi.yml`** \u2014 updated the `identityKey` field description/example to the new SHA-256 form.\n\nBoth acceptance oracles pass: `coreIdentityKeyIsSha256Hex` (64-hex shape) and `coreRejectsDuplicateIdentity` (identical identity \u2192 409).",
